17. Recommended three Round 3 improvements
Choose three substantial additions and describe them as future work until implemented:
1. Visual governance: compare supported creative assets with approved logos/visual rules and surface explainable issues for human review.
2. Expanded document support: more robust PDF/DOCX extraction, source locations and document review workflow.
3. Governance and organisational insights: stronger role-based permissions, rule versioning, reviewer comments/auditability, and improved recurrence reporting.
Do not promise all these as Round 2 functionality.
18. Codex working protocol
Codex should work in small, auditable steps and protect existing useful work.
Phase 0: repository audit — no edits
1. Inspect the full repository and current framework.
2. Identify existing working screens, functions, dependencies, database and current deploy path.
3. Map the actual code against this PRD.
4. Run existing tests/lint/build if available and report the observed state.
5. Propose the smallest safe implementation plan, with a file-by-file change list.
6. Do not rewrite or delete project files at this phase.
Phase 1: establish the working vertical slice
Implement or repair this path first: load sample brand → submit/paste draft → deterministic check → evidence-linked finding → accept/edit/reject → persisted revision. Test it before moving on.
Phase 2: AI integration
Add Gemini with structured output, Pydantic validation, approved-source validation, timeouts, retries only where safe, and fallback behaviour. Test API errors explicitly.
Phase 3: uploads and history
Add supported document extraction and review history without breaking paste input. Add file limits and extraction status.
Phase 4: recurrence and manager alerts
Track matching confirmed findings across drafts and implement configurable thresholds. Test both alert and non-alert cases.
Phase 5: review for submission
Run the acceptance tests, inspect the deployed link as a fresh user, document actual limitations and update README/submission materials.
Codex rules
- Do not claim a feature works until its code path has been exercised.
- Do not build a decorative empty dashboard instead of functional review.
- Do not redesign or rewrite the whole app without evidence it is necessary.
- Do not hard-code secrets, fake AI results, fake sources or fake test results.
- Do not allow unsupported AI output to become approved brand knowledge.
- Do not silently change the product name or core stack; explain any proposed change first.
- For every implementation phase, provide: files changed, behaviour added, tests run, observed outcomes, known limitations, and exact next step.
